{
  "source_db": "databases/source.db",
  "target_db": "databases/target.db",
  "scope": ["customers", "orders", "order_items", "payments", "addresses"],
  "tables": {
    "customers": {"source_rows": 8000, "target_rows": 8000, "equal": true},
    "orders": {"source_rows": 50200, "target_rows": 50200, "equal": true},
    "order_items": {"source_rows": 40000, "target_rows": 40000, "equal": true},
    "payments": {"source_rows": 12000, "target_rows": 12000, "equal": true},
    "addresses": {"source_rows": 9000, "target_rows": 9000, "equal": true}
  },
  "matched": true,
  "notes": "Basis: logical rows-on-record. Per the contract, orders-on-record = active (orders) + archived (orders_archive) for BOTH snapshots. Source orders = 50000 active + 200 archived = 50200; target orders = 50000 active + 200 archived = 50200 (no id overlap between the two tables in either DB, so no double-counting). reconcile.py reports only the physical 'orders' table (50000 vs 50000) and does not include orders_archive, so its raw output understates rows-on-record; it does not decide sign-off. All other in-scope tables use their single physical table count. Every in-scope table has source == target rows-on-record, so matched=true. Read-only reconciliation; no databases, migrations, scope, or queries were modified."
}
